
# Task 4: Floating-Point Arithmetic and Numerical Errors

## 1. Objective

The aim is to understand floating-point errors and calculate numerical errors using Python.

## 2. Inputs

The program uses 0.1, 0.2 and 0.3 for floating-point calculations. It also uses 1.414 as an approximation of the square root of 2.

## 3. Mathematical Operations

The program performs addition and calculates absolute and relative errors.

Absolute error = |True value - Approximate value|

Relative error = Absolute error / |True value|

## 4. Original Output

The addition of 0.1 and 0.2 produced 0.30000000000000004 instead of exactly 0.3.

The exact comparison returned False, while np.isclose() returned True.

The absolute error was 0.0002135624, and the relative error was 0.0001510114.

## 5. Modified Input

I changed the approximate value from 1.414 to 1.4142.

## 6. Observation

The modified approximation produced a smaller absolute error than the original approximation.


## 7. Interpretation

Increasing the accuracy of an approximate value reduces numerical error.

## 8. Related Real-World Problem

Financial and scientific applications use numerical calculations that require accurate results.

## 9. Assumptions

The square root of 2 is treated as the true reference value. The approximation is given to three or four decimal places.

## 10. New Input Data

The new approximate value is 1.4142 instead of 1.414.

## 11. Output

The modified absolute error was 0.0000135624, showing an improvement in accuracy.

## 12. Interpretation of the Modified Problem

The new approximation is closer to the true value. This demonstrates how numerical accuracy can be improved by using more precise values.
